\DOC derive_nonschematic_coinductive_relations

\TYPE {derive_nonschematic_coinductive_relations : term -> thm}

\SYNOPSIS
Deduce coinductive definitions properties from an explicit assignment.

\DESCRIBE
Given a set of clauses as given to {new_coinductive_definition}, the call
{derive_nonschematic_coinductive_relations} will introduce explicit
equational constraints (``definitions'', though only assumptions of the
theorem, not actually constant definitions) that allow it to deduce the rule,
coinduction and cases theorems. Each relation is defined as the union of all
relations consistent with the rules. The theorem will in general have
additional `monotonicity' hypotheses, but these may be removable by
{prove_monotonicity_hyps}. None of the arguments are treated as schematic.

\FAILURE
Fails if the format of the clauses is wrong.

\EXAMPLE
Here a predicate on sequences says that {P} holds at every position:
{
  # (prove_monotonicity_hyps o derive_nonschematic_coinductive_relations)
      `!s:num->A. P(s 0) /\ always(\n. s(SUC n)) ==> always s`;;
  val it : thm =
    always =
    (\a. ?always'.
             (!a. always' a ==> P (a 0) /\ always' (\n. a (SUC n))) /\
             always' a)
    |- (!s. P (s 0) /\ always (\n. s (SUC n)) ==> always s) /\
       (!always'.
            (!a. always' a ==> P (a 0) /\ always' (\n. a (SUC n)))
            ==> (!a. always' a ==> always a)) /\
       (!a. always a <=> P (a 0) /\ always (\n. a (SUC n)))
}

\USES
This is a lower-level function underlying {new_coinductive_definition} and
{prove_coinductive_relations_exist}, and is not normally used directly.

\SEEALSO
derive_nonschematic_inductive_relations, new_coinductive_definition,
prove_coinductive_relations_exist, prove_monotonicity_hyps.

\ENDDOC
